 "case:briefing.0": "Hafod Studios, mid-Wales. Wednesday 30 October 2024. A hill farm above the Wye, at the end of a single-track lane: a farmhouse, an old stone granary, and a long barn that has been a recording studio since 1979. Half the records you remember from the nineties were made here.",
 "case:briefing.1": "In the early hours of Sunday 27 October, Rhys Morgan, 52, the singer of Halflight, fell backwards down the stone steps outside the granary where he was sleeping. His smartwatch felt the fall and rang 999 by itself. He was dead before the ambulance arrived.",
 "case:briefing.2": "It was meant to be a comeback. Halflight split up in 1998; this week, for the first time in twenty-six years, the three of them were back in one room, recording, with a documentary camera watching. Their old hit, ‘Small Hours’, has been everywhere this year.",
 "case:briefing.3": "The police have the documentary’s footage: Dean Pritchard, the band’s guitarist, shouting at Rhys at the foot of those steps and shoving him in the chest. Dean says he wrote the music of ‘Small Hours’, and he never got the credit. He told the police he had gone to bed; then that he had left Rhys alive ‘at about quarter to two’. The watch felt the fall at 01:41. Dean has been arrested on suspicion of murder and released on bail. The CPS is deciding whether to charge him, and a tabloid has run the pictures under the headline ‘THE SHOVE’.",
 "case:briefing.4": "Dean’s daughter, Ffion, has hired you. ‘Dad’s been angry with Rhys for thirty years. He’s also the only person who never stopped loving him. He didn’t do this.’ Huw Bevan, who owns Hafod, has given you the run of the place, and everyone who was there that night is still close by.",
 "case:epilogue.0": "Eddie Cole said nothing to anybody except his solicitor. He didn’t need to. The 999 recording, Dana Whitlock’s call log, Bryn Lewis’s statement and two brass corners from the ash pan of the lounge stove were enough, and he was charged with murder. The police cancelled Dean’s bail the same afternoon, and the CPS dropped him.",
 "case:epilogue.1": "He had planned it from the Thursday, when Rhys first said he couldn’t sell ‘Small Hours’ as his own. He moved the Los Angeles call to Saturday at 17:20 Pacific time — 01:20 at Hafod, in summer time — because Huw had written on the whiteboard that the clocks went back at two. He knew Rhys’s watch would ring 999 and stamp the time. All he had to do was be seen at the first twenty to two and wait in the dark for the second. He gave the landing bulb a quarter-turn on his way in. He never told anyone a false time. He simply let them believe there was only one of each.",
 "case:epilogue.2": "What he hadn’t counted on was Los Angeles. Dana rang to confirm Monday at 6.43 their time, 01:43 at Hafod, while Eddie was kneeling beside Rhys with the book in his hand. His phone played the riff he had loved for thirty years into an open line to the ambulance service. On Sunday morning he lit the stove and burned the book, and thirty years of words went with it. The letter Rhys wrote on Saturday afternoon survived only as dents in the page beneath it, on Huw’s notepad.",
 "case:epilogue.3": "Westwater walked away from the sale. PRS amended the registration of ‘Small Hours’ to Pritchard/Morgan, from first publication. They did it on the strength of a pencil rubbing, and of a cassette from 1995 on which a twenty-two-year-old Rhys says, ‘That’s it, Deano. That’s the one.’ Cole Music went into administration before Christmas.",
 "case:epilogue.4": "Carys paid back the sixty thousand pounds, and asked Dean to play at the funeral. Freya gave the Banner’s eight thousand pounds to a music project for children in Pill, gave every frame she had shot to Dean’s solicitor, and turned down the streaming service’s new budget; her film is about two friends now, and it takes its time. Bryn went back to his meetings on the Tuesday and started counting his days again from one. Huw fitted a handrail to the granary steps, and now puts the clocks back himself.",
 "case:epilogue.5": "At the funeral in Newport, Dean played ‘Small Hours’ alone, on the five-string guitar he wrote it on. It’s a song about staying up until the town is asleep and the hour comes round again. He sang it in Rhys’s key.",
